          "check": "tier_integrity",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "E13 (strong correlation between exclusion and crime) is cited as 'measurable' but it is a research finding/synthesis presented as an established fact. The underlying claim is correlational research, which is better classified as 'projected' (contested causal inference). Not blocking because correlation itself is measurable, but the framing edges toward implying causation."
        },
        {
          "check": "tier_integrity",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "E27 (PRU placements often become permanent) is cited as 'measurable' but is sourced partly from dera.ioe.ac.uk (older academic report) and ippr-org \u2014 a mix of academic and advocacy. The claim is plausible but 'often' is an unquantified assertion from sources that include advocacy. Should be 'projected'."
        },
        {
          "check": "source_support",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "E15 (33 percentage point custody increase) comes from bi.team (Behavioural Insights Team / Nuffield Foundation report) \u2014 a reputable quasi-academic source, but the claim is stated as 'has been shown' which implies established consensus. The verdict correctly marks it 'projected' and the rationale notes causality is debated, so this is handled, but the claim text itself ('has been shown') slightly overstates certainty."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "The verdict is well-constructed. The mixed direction is genuinely supported by claims on both sides: short-term school safety gain (stated, plausible) vs. long-term crime-pipeline risk (multi-source, robust correlation). The rationale correctly caveats the E14 figure as single-source/indicative. No party-identity language or motive attribution detected. No lever leakage \u2014 the verdict judges effect on safety/crime outcomes, not the exclusion policy as an end in itself. Minor tier classification issues flagged as warnings only; none are blocking."
    },
